# **Unveiling the Origins: Raw Materials for Lithium-Ion Batteries** 

## Abstract

Originally developed for a first-year assignment in CTS 2000 at the University of Guelph, *Unveiling the Origins: Raw Materials for Lithium-Ion Batteries* is an interactive [StoryMapJS](https://storymap.knightlab.com/) exploring the geographical origins of four critical materials used in lithium-ion batteries:[^1]

| Materials explored |
| :---- |
| Lithium | 
| Cobalt | 
| Graphite | 
| Manganese |

The assignment asked us to select a technology and explore it through a series of projects, including a map. I chose e-bikes, focusing specifically on the materials required to produce their batteries. What began as an attempt to trace the complex life cycle of lithium-ion batteries gradually developed into a more focused exploration of where four key materials are extracted, how they are sourced, and the human and environmental implications of their production.

Through a series of geographical locations, the map begins to move beyond identifying countries of extraction to highlight particular mining sites and the experiences of surrounding communities. In doing so, it invites readers to consider the often-overlooked origins of a technology typically associated with convenience and environmental “progress.”

My original brief reflection on the research and map-making process can be found on my [blog](https://darceyehilliard.wixsite.com/my-site-2/post/unveiling-the-origins-raw-materials-for-lithium-ion-batteries).



