## Author's Note

At first glance, e-bikes might seem somewhat removed from the [theme](https://cts-guelph.github.io/ctrl-alt-dh/cfp-2026.html) of digital anxieties, which we commonly associate with AI, surveillance, and our increasingly online lives. However, I think this project speaks to another form of technological unease: the difficulty of fully understanding the systems, materials, labour, and human experiences that make our everyday technologies possible.

E-bikes offer a useful example of this contradiction. They can provide more accessible transportation, encourage an offline lifestyle, and offer environmental benefits—a seemingly “green” technology. Yet their production, like that of many technologies, depends on global supply chains with significant social and environmental consequences. The benefits of a technology are not necessarily experienced equally by those who use it and those involved in creating it.

One of the challenges I encountered while developing this map was deciding what story to tell. The life cycle of a lithium-ion battery is enormously complex and, in many ways, hidden from view. I gradually narrowed my focus to four materials, their primary extraction locations, and the experiences of people living and working in those regions. Even then, finding publicly accessible information about specific mining sites and their surrounding communities, rather than simply identifying countries of extraction, proved difficult. This process reinforced how easily the origins of our technologies can become obscured. We often encounter them as finished products, removed from the geographical and human contexts that make their existence possible.

This brings us to some important questions: Who benefits from technological progress? Who bears its costs? And who gets to participate in it?

I think navigating digital anxieties, and technological anxieties more broadly, requires us to look beyond our immediate interactions with technology. Greater awareness of the systems behind our technologies may both alleviate and exacerbate our anxieties: understanding them can help us make more informed choices, but it can also reveal uncomfortable realities that are difficult to reconcile with our everyday reliance on them. Nevertheless, I think it is important that we make space for the human experiences that might otherwise remain overlooked.

This project does not attempt to resolve these contradictions, nor does it suggest that the benefits of e-bikes are automatically negated by the challenges associated with their production. Rather, I hope it encourages readers to consider the complicated relationships we have with technologies, particularly those we have grown so accustomed to using, by tracing them back to their origins.

